     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. I can't verify the indication, and I know of no condition or stage of treatment where 50 mg of amlodipine daily is appropriate. That is five times the labeled maximum of 10 mg per day. Amlodipine has a long half-life of 30 to 50 hours, so a dose this size would accumulate. The risk is profound hypotension and shock that can be hard to reverse, and it would fall on a 70 kg patient with no organ impairment.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **If the dose was meant to be lower**, tell me and I'll enter 5 mg or 10 mg right away.\n- **If more blood pressure control is needed**, adding a second agent is usually safer than going past 10 mg. Examples are an ACE inhibitor or ARB, or a thiazide-like diuretic. I can enter one of those if you tell me which.\n- **If you believe 50 mg is truly required**, please discuss it with a clinical pharmacist or attending. They can place it through the hospital's formal override process, with the rationale documented and monitoring in place."
      }
     ],
